  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nLate Tonight and Monday Morning \n\nThe 19.12z models are in general agreement that a mesoscale\nconvective complex will develop over northern and western Minnesota\nthis evening, and then they weaken this complex overnight as \nthe nocturnal jet weakens toward day break. While the models are\nin agreement of the evolution of the system, they diverremains\nin the region throughge on the track of this complex. Some move\nthis system along and north of Interstate 94. Others move it \nsoutheast through the Upper Mississippi or even further \nsouthwest through the western two- thirds of Iowa. The severe \nweather threat for our area is highly dependent on how early the\nconvection is able to move into the Upper Mississippi River \nValley. If the convection can move into the area prior to 4 am, \nthere could be a damaging wind threat. If it moves in after that\ntime, the severe weather threat looks to be greatly diminished.\n\nAs far as smoke, the latest HRRR-NCEP shows the near-surface\nsmoke will be gradually improving west of the Mississippi River\nlate this afternoon and evening and in southwest and central\nWisconsin during the overnight.\n\nLow temperatures will be in the mid- and upper 60s.\n\nMonday Afternoon and Evening\n\nThe convection during the afternoon is highly dependent on where\nthe thunderstorm complex tracks overnight and where its remnant\noutflow boundary ultimately ends up. \n\nIn the more southern solutions, this outflow boundary will \nlikely end up south of Interstate 90 and the convection would \ninitiate more into the evening. With the cold front more \nparallel, the convection would orientate itself into one or \nmultiple lines segments. Meanwhile, further north of Interstate\n90, the orientation with the cold front is more perpendicular \nand this would result in discrete supercells with all hazards \npossible.\n\nIn the more northern solutions, the outflow boundary would be \ncloser to Interstates 90 and 94. In this scenario, the convection\ncould continue throughout the afternoon and evening due to the \nlack of a cap. There could be a line or multiple line segments \nsouth of Interstate 90 with the damaging winds being the primary\nconcern, and discrete to scattered supercells along the cold \nfront during the late afternoon and evening with all severe \nweather threats possible.\n\nDepending on how it fast it clears out after the morning\nconvection in northeast Iowa, there could be a need for a Heat \nAdvisory. The current forecast heat indices are 100 to 105F. \nThese could be lower if the outflow boundary from tonremains in\nthe region throughight's convection ends up further south into \nIowa. These would be higher if outflow boundary does not get as \nfar south. Due to this uncertainty, held off issuing one for \nnow.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the lower 80s to lower 90s.\n\nMonday Overnight\n\nThe latest HRRR-NCEP shows some increase in the surface smoke\nconcentrations in the wake of the front where the subsidence is\nthe greatest. However, they would likely only last up to 3 \nhours. At this time, thinking that these concentrations are not\nenough to impact surface visibilities.\n\nLow temperatures will be in the mid- and upper 60s.",
